# Compiled Dynamic libraries
*.so
*.dylib
*.dll

# ...EXCEPT the runnable corpus's PREBUILT SECOND IMAGES, which are SOURCE, not
# build output. `examples/c/environ_alias_object_identity/` ships two
# gcc-built `.so`s (x86_64 + aarch64, from `env_probe_so.c` in the same
# directory, build command recorded there) because an OBJECT-IDENTITY property
# cannot be witnessed by ONE image — the example needs a second image DSS did
# not build, and shipping it prebuilt means the corpus needs no host
# cross-compiler at test time (D-LK-ELF-COPY-RELOC-CLAIMS-ONE-NAME-OF-AN-ALIAS-SET).
# ★ WITHOUT THIS NEGATION the `.so`s are silently un-committable and the example
# fails on every fresh clone. It fails LOUD there (exit 10, the fail-closed
# witness check refuses to skip), but loud-on-every-clone is not shipped
# coverage. Scoped to this one directory on purpose: `*.so` must keep ignoring
# real build output everywhere else. `.gitattributes` declares these paths
# `binary` so the `examples/** text eol=lf` rule cannot rewrite their bytes.
!examples/**/*.so

# Fortran module files
*.mod
*.smod

# Compiled Static libraries
*.lai
*.la
*.a
*.lib

# ★ BSD `ar` archive fixtures (D-FF1-AR-BSD-VARIANT), the exact mirror of the
# `!examples/**/*.so` negation above and for the same reason. `tests/ffi/
# test_binary_reader_ar_bsd.cpp` reads two REAL archives -- one from Apple's
# `ar` on macOS, one from `llvm-ar --format=bsd` -- because a hand-authored BSD
# archive would only test the reader against its author's reading of the format
# and the two would agree by construction. Neither can be regenerated at test
# time: the Apple one needs a Mac, and this repo's `ar` WRITER emits GNU only.
# ★ WITHOUT THIS NEGATION both fixtures are silently un-committable and every
# assertion in that file dies on a fresh clone -- loudly (the tests assert the
# bytes are non-empty), but loud-on-every-clone is not shipped coverage.
# Scoped to this one directory on purpose: `*.a` must keep ignoring real build
# output everywhere else. `.gitattributes` already declares `tests/**/data/**`
# `binary`, so no text rule can rewrite their bytes. Provenance + md5s for both
# are recorded in `tests/ffi/data/README-bsd-archives.md`.
!tests/ffi/data/*.a

# ★ FOREIGN-UNWIND archive fixtures
# (D-LK-MERGED-FOREIGN-FUNCTIONS-CARRY-NO-UNWIND-INFO-IN-THE-IMAGE), the same
# negation as the BSD one above and for the same reason.
# `examples/c/merged_foreign_unwind_carry` links a PREBUILT `gcc -c` + `ar rcs`
# archive whose members carry a real `.eh_frame`, because the subject of that
# row is a FOREIGN encoding: an archive DSS built itself would be reading back
# this project's own writer and could not fail the way the row describes. The
# manifest's `containerWitness` is the literal `.eh_frame`, so a fixture
# regenerated with `-fno-asynchronous-unwind-tables` fails the manifest instead
# of passing vacuously.
# ★ WITHOUT THIS NEGATION both fixtures are silently un-committable, and the
# example then fails on every machine except the one that built them — which is
# worse than the pre-P32 state, because the failure looks like a defect in the
# carry rather than a missing input. Scoped to this one directory on purpose.
# Provenance + md5s are in the example's `expected.json`.
!tests/link/data/*.a

# Executables
*.exe
*.out
*.app

# debug information files
*.dwo

# Build directory
.temp/
.cache/
# Compiler cache. The CI `ccache-action` materialises `.ccache/<hex>/CACHEDIR.TAG`
# INSIDE the workspace, and on a Windows runner ccache writes those tags with CRLF.
# They are untracked and were therefore NOT ignored, so `line_endings_guard` (which
# scans `git ls-files --others --exclude-standard`) convicted 16 of them and failed
# the windows-msvc-release leg — on bytes no human wrote and nothing would ever
# commit. Ignoring is the correct classification, not a silencer: a content-addressed
# compiler cache is build output, never source.
.ccache/
build/
build-wsl/
# Ad-hoc out-of-source build dirs (e.g. build-rel / build-dbg used for
# timed before/after measurements and per-config canary builds).
build-*/
# D-RUNTIME-DSS-SHIPS-NO-IMPLEMENTATION-HALF: the shipped RUNTIME's object cache.
# `src/dss-config/runtime/platform/` is self-describing — `src/` holds authored C,
# `dist/` holds generated objects — and nothing generated is ever interleaved with
# anything authored. A generated tree living INSIDE `src/` with nothing ignoring it
# is exactly how object files end up committed.
#
# ★★ THE PATH IS ANCHORED, AND THAT IS NOT STYLE. A bare `dist` glob would match
# any directory of that name anywhere in the repo, and — worse in the direction
# that bites — the class of accident this repo already paid for: an UNANCHORED
# `build*` exclude silently matched `src/program/build_scripts.cpp`, and a gate leg
# was configured against a tree missing a changed `.cpp`. Anchoring keeps the rule
# to the one tree it is about, so a future source file whose name merely starts
# with `dist` cannot disappear.
/src/dss-config/runtime/platform/dist/
tmp_probe/
# Ad-hoc agent/probe scratch at repo root (repro programs, throwaway bash
# scripts) — working files, never committed. (Prefer the out-of-repo session
# scratchpad; this catches anything that lands here anyway.)
scratchpad/
# SESSION HAND-OFF NOTES — operator ruling 2026-09-04: PUNCTUAL, NEVER COMMITTED.
# A `.plans/_next-cycle-*.md` is written when a session ends mid-arc so the NEXT
# session can read what was prepared and then delete it. It is a message between
# sessions, not a project record — the durable account of a cycle belongs in
# `_handoff.md` and in the anchor rows, which are versioned.
# ⚠ IT LIVES IN `.plans/` ON PURPOSE, because a next session opens `.plans/` and
# the out-of-repo scratchpad is keyed to a session id it will not have. Ignoring
# it here is what keeps it out of `git status`, so an explicit-path stage cannot
# sweep it into a cycle commit the way one already did once.
.plans/_next-cycle-*.md
# DSS CLI default output dir (target-spec-named subdirs of compiled binaries
# from ad-hoc `dsscp <src>` runs; not a build-system dir).
target/
# `.dss-project.json` `dependsOn` checkout cache. ★ THIS DIRECTORY BELONGS TO
# THE PROJECT BEING COMPILED, NOT TO THIS COMPILER: dsscp creates
# `.dss-deps/<name>/` beside the CONSUMING project's own manifest — in the
# user's repo, wherever their `.dss-project.json` lives — never in the
# compiler's tree or in a shared/global location. A build of someone else's
# project must never write inside this repo.
#
# The entry is here only because THIS repo also contains `.dss-project.json`
# manifests of its own (examples/, real-examples/, and the runners' scratch
# trees), so a `dependsOn` example compiled in place would drop the cache
# inside our working tree. It is ACQUIRED content, never source: reproducible
# from the manifest's pins, and committing it would vendor someone else's
# history into this repo. (See docs/project-config-spec.md §2.6.)
.dss-deps/
# ⓘ 2026-08-19: `!scripts/build/` stood here, because the unanchored `build/`
# rule above matches at ANY depth and swallowed the local-build script's
# directory. The scripts/ consolidation renamed that directory to
# `scripts/local-build/`, which no ignore rule matches, so the negation was
# removed rather than retargeted: a negation that negates nothing reads as
# protection and provides none.

# CTest leftover when ctest is invoked from the repo root (e.g. inside
# a CI step that cd's wrong) — these are throwaway run artifacts.
Testing/

# Test-scratch directories created by examples_runner + integrated_tests
# during per-example compile/run cycles. Throwaway working dirs that
# happen to land at repo-root when ctest runs without a build-dir cwd.
test-scratch/

# Python bytecode cache (e.g. scripts/refresh_landing_log/refresh_landing_log.py)
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Claude Code per-user local settings (allow-list, denied tools, etc.).
# Project-wide skills under .claude/skills/ stay tracked.
.claude/settings.local.json
.claude/scheduled_tasks.lock
# Agent worktrees: throwaway checkouts an agent creates for an isolated build.
# ★ THIS RULE WAS ONLY IN `.git/info/exclude`, WHICH IS PER-CLONE AND NEVER
# COMMITTED, so it protected exactly one machine. ✔MEASURED: the arm64 VPS leg
# failed `check-line-endings.sh` on
# `.claude/worktrees/agent-<id>/{LICENSE,VERSION}` — files carrying CRLF inside a
# vendored checkout that this repo does not own and must not rewrite. The guard
# was RIGHT to refuse untracked CR-bearing files; what was wrong is that agent
# scratch was visible to it at all on every host but mine. ⚠ Note the shape of
# the near-miss: the prescribed remedy (convert the file to LF) would have
# corrupted somebody else's vendored tree to silence a guard about our own.
.claude/worktrees/

# ★★ THE SANCTIONED HOME FOR LANE WORKTREES — operator ruling 2026-08-26:
# "I want the worktrees implementation to be inside the project root, .worktrees
# directory (where 100% of it's internal content ignored by .gitignore). This way
# we stop contaminating builds outside repository bounds."
#
# Before this, lanes took worktrees at absolute paths OUTSIDE the repo (C:/dssp40k,
# C:/dssp40l, …). That kept the tree clean but scattered full checkouts and their
# build dirs across the filesystem, where nothing owns them and no guard can see
# them. Inside the root they are enumerable, and `git worktree list` plus
# `git worktree prune` become the inventory instead of somebody's memory.
#
# ★ THE ANCHORING IS NOT STYLE. A bare `worktrees/` would match any directory of
# that name at any depth — including a future `examples/**/worktrees/` fixture —
# and the repo has already paid once for an unanchored rule (`build*` silently
# swallowed `src/program/build_scripts.cpp` and a gate leg configured against a
# tree missing a changed `.cpp`). The leading `/` keeps this to the one directory
# it is about.
#
# ⚠ THIS LINE IS WHAT KEEPS LANE CHECKOUTS OFF EVERY GATE HOST, and that is a
# REQUIREMENT, not a nicety — operator, same day: "worktrees MUST be ignored by
# ALL host copies to run legs". Since 2026-08-26 the carriages derive their
# exclude list from git (`scripts/carriage-excludes/`), so this rule — and only
# this rule — is what stops N full repo copies riding to macOS and the arm64 VPS
# on every push. ✔MEASURED: the same accident under `.claude/worktrees/` put
# 9,638 files on the macOS host, and the examples runner globs `examples/<lang>/*`,
# so a gate host holding one can run somebody's uncommitted corpus and report the
# result as the cycle's. `.worktrees/` is therefore ALSO pinned in that script's
# `MUST_NEVER_TRAVEL` floor, which re-asks git and REFUSES the carriage if this
# line ever disappears — the rule is asserted on every leg, not trusted.
#
# ⚠ `git clean -fd` (what `scripts/leg-tree/` restore runs) does NOT delete
# ignored paths, so a live worktree survives a leg restore. `-fdx` WOULD destroy
# it mid-build; the dss-cycle skill forbids `-fdx` for exactly this reason.
/.worktrees/

# Agent-manager tooling and third-party dependency trees. THE SAME SHAPE AS THE
# RULE ABOVE, and it cost the same way: `.kilo/` was covered only by
# `.git/info/exclude` plus a `.kilo/.gitignore` the tool writes itself, both
# per-clone, so every OTHER machine saw it as ordinary repository content.
# ✔MEASURED 2026-08-26: the arm64 VPS gate host held `.kilo/` — 3,671 files, 61 MB,
# 3,667 of them `node_modules` — because every carriage excluded `node_modules`
# ANCHORED at the top level and `.kilo/node_modules` is not at the top level.
# ★ Since 2026-08-26 the carriages derive their exclude list from git
# (`scripts/carriage-excludes/`), so a rule written HERE is what keeps a tree off
# every gate host, and a rule written only in `.git/info/exclude` protects one.
# D-SCRIPT-CARRIAGE-EXCLUDES-ARE-A-HAND-LIST-AND-MISS-NESTED-IGNORED-TREES
.kilo/
node_modules/
.venv/

#.plans/ -- Not for now, but will be used when first minimal implementation is done and we start working on the next steps.

# Operator connection data for scripts/ssh-*/ — hosts, logins, key PATHS.
# NEVER tracked: this repo is slated to go public (PR #37) and these name real machines.
# ★ Connection data + key material for the cross-host tooling.
#   ⚠ THE ORIGINAL RULE HERE WAS "PATHS ONLY, no private key material" — that changed
#   on 2026-08-17 by operator instruction, and the reason is worth keeping: a key
#   referenced as `$HOME/.ssh/<name>` resolves to a DIFFERENT directory in WSL, Git Bash
#   and PowerShell, so a host was reachable or not depending on which shell you started
#   in. ✔MEASURED: the arm64 VPS key existed only under WSL's $HOME, making the carriage
#   work from WSL and fail from the other two. `.secrets/` is the SAME directory in all
#   three, so the key lives here and the carriage FINDS it — "you must be able to get
#   everything from the tool scripts … never manually".
.secrets/

# ★★ BELT AND BRACES, and deliberately BROADER than `.secrets/`. Key material and
#   connection data must never reach a commit even if it is written somewhere nobody
#   anticipated — a rule that only covers the directory people are supposed to use is a
#   rule that protects the disciplined case and misses the accident. This repo is slated
#   to go public (PR #37), so the cost of a miss is unbounded and irreversible.
#   ⛔ Do NOT narrow these to a directory. If a legitimate file is caught, un-ignore it
#   by an explicit `!` exception with a reason, never by weakening the pattern.
*.env
*.key
*.pem
*.p12
*.pfx
id_rsa
id_ecdsa
id_ed25519
*_rsa
*_ed25519
# Explicit exceptions: template/example files that carry NO secret, only field names.
!*.env.example
!*.env.template
